{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CgmfKfJngJqDDZJKm2xU5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have no docking, scoring, or structure-retrieval tool in this session — no AutoDock/Vina, no RDKit, no PDB fetch, no shell. So I cannot compute a binding affinity for you. What follows is from my own knowledge, flagged as such, plus what you'd need to get a real number.\n\n## Why a single \"predicted affinity\" is the wrong target here\n\n**2HHB** is Fermi, Perutz, Shih & Fourme's human **deoxy**haemoglobin at 1.74 Å (J Mol Biol 1984, 175:159–174) — the T-state α₂β₂ tetramer with four protoporphyrin-IX/Fe²⁺ hemes and no bound drug. For an allosteric effector it's actually a sensible template, because the 2,3-BPG cleft on the β₁β₂ dyad (βVal1, βHis2, βLys82, βHis143) is open only in the T state. That cleft, not the heme pocket, is where small aromatic acids (bezafibrate, efaproxiral/RSR13, clofibric acid derivatives) dock.\n\nBut aspirin's real interaction with haemoglobin is **covalent, not equilibrium binding**. Aspirin transfers its acetyl group to haemoglobin lysines — this is the basis of the old work on acetylated HbS and sickling inhibition (Klotz & Tam, PNAS 1973). β-chain lysines are the reported targets; I recall βLys-59 and βLys-144 among them, but I'd treat the specific residue assignments as needing a literature check, since different studies report different primary sites. A docking score models none of this: it scores the non-covalent pre-reaction complex, which is a weak and transient thing.\n\n## What the non-covalent number looks like, approximately\n\nFrom my recollection of the spectroscopic/fluorescence-quenching literature on aspirin and salicylate with human haemoglobin, association constants fall around **10³–10⁴ M⁻¹**, i.e. **Kd ≈ 0.1–1 mM**, ΔG ≈ **−4.5 to −6 kcal/mol**. I cannot give you a specific citation or confirm those values without database access, so treat the range as an order-of-magnitude recollection rather than a sourced measurement. That is consistent with pharmacology: plasma salicylate at therapeutic doses is 0.1–2 mM, roughly the same scale as the Kd, which is why occupancy is low and the slow covalent reaction dominates.\n\nA Vina-class docking run on the β-cleft of 2HHB would very likely report something in the **−5 to −6.5 kcal/mol** band. Two reasons not to believe that as an affinity: it is near the noise floor where Vina cannot discriminate real weak binders from decoys, and aspirin (carboxylate pKa ≈ 3.5) is **anionic at pH 7.4** — docking the neutral form systematically overestimates the score. Ligand efficiency would be ~0.45 kcal/mol per heavy atom (13 heavy atoms, MW 180.2), which looks respectable only because the molecule is tiny.\n\n## If you want an actual computed value\n\nEverything needed is specified; you just need a tool I don't have:\n\n- Ligand: aspirin, SMILES `CC(=O)Oc1ccccc1C(=O)O`, deprotonated carboxylate at pH 7.4\n- Receptor: 2HHB, keep the tetramer (the site is inter-subunit), strip waters, keep hemes\n- Site: β-cleft centroid between βVal1/βHis2/βLys82/βHis143 of chains B and D; a 22 Å box\n- Comparator to sanity-check the scoring function: dock bezafibrate or efaproxiral into the same box — these have measured Hb affinities and should score better than aspirin\n\nIf you have a docking or MMGBSA tool available on your side, I'll prepare the input files and interpret the output. For the question you're actually likely asking — does aspirin meaningfully occupy haemoglobin — a non-covalent docking score won't answer it; a covalent/acetylation-rate analysis would.\n\n## ANSWER\n\nUNABLE to compute a predicted affinity: no docking or affinity-prediction tool exists in this session.\n\nFrom knowledge, not computation: aspirin binds haemoglobin **weakly** — non-covalent Kd on the order of **0.1–1 mM** (ΔG ≈ −4.5 to −6 kcal/mol), a recollected literature range I could not verify here. A Vina-type score on the T-state β-cleft of 2HHB would likely read −5 to −6.5 kcal/mol, which is within that method's noise floor and inflated if aspirin is docked neutral rather than as the pH-7.4 anion. The pharmacologically real interaction is **covalent acetylation of β-chain lysines**, which no docking score represents."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":18927,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":18927},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"47dd00ef-0f67-4786-81ba-c123edbd60d4","uuid":"246bf773-c362-44d3-ae02-5ce22bc9e481","timestamp":"2026-11-06T12:19:33.493Z","request_id":"req_011CgmfKezT8oHgd559V1Lea"}
